"},{"id":"p3","kind":"path","question":"When `template.format(user=user, comment=safe_comment)` reaches the `{user:link}` field, what does `EscapeFormatter.format_field` do with the `User` instance, and why is the returned `<a href=...>` markup not escaped again before being spliced into the result?","key":"`format_field` sees `hasattr(value, "__html_format__")` first (line 340), so it calls `user.__html_format__('link')`, which returns `Markup('<a href="/user/3"><span class="user">Alice &#34;The &lt;b&gt;Great&lt;/b&gt;&#34;</span></a>')`. The result is then passed through `str(self.escape(rv))` (line 354); since `rv` is a `Markup`, `escape` takes the `__html__` branch and returns it unchanged, so the tags survive while the name stays escaped. `User.__format__`/`str.format` default formatting is never used.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:339-354","src/markupsafe/__init__.py:313-315"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
    sys.path.insert(0, 'src')
    import markupsafe
from markupsafe import Markup
class User:
    def __init__(self, id, name):
        self.id = id
        self.name = name
    def __html_format__(self, spec):
        if spec == 'link':
            return Markup('<a href="/user/{}">{}</a>').format(self.id, self.__html__())
        elif spec:
            raise ValueError('Invalid format spec')
        return self.__html__()
    def __html__(self):
        return Markup('<span class="user">{0}</span>').format(self.name)
u = User(3, 'Alice "The <b>Great</b>"')
out = Markup('{user:link}').format(user=u)
assert str(out) == '<a href="/user/3"><span class="user">Alice &#34;The &lt;b&gt;Great&lt;/b&gt;&#34;</span></a>', out
"},{"id":"p4","kind":"path","question":"The `{comment}` field of the template receives `safe_comment`, a `Markup`. Which branch of `EscapeFormatter.format_field` handles it, and what would happen instead if the template had been written `{comment:>40}`?","key":"`Markup` itself defines `__html_format__`, so the first branch is taken: `Markup.__html_format__('')` is called; since `format_spec` is empty it simply returns `self`, and `str(self.escape(rv))` leaves the already-escaped text alone, giving `&lt;script&gt;alert(document.cookie);&lt;/script&gt;`. With `{comment:>40}` the spec is non-empty, so `Markup.__html_format__` raises `ValueError("Unsupported format specification for Markup.")` — Markup does not support format specs at all.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:325-329","src/markupsafe/__init__.py:339-354"],"check":"import sys
