"},{"id":"g1","kind":"general","question":"Markup overrides many `str` methods. Which of them escape their arguments and which deliberately do not, and what is the stated rationale for the ones that don't?","key":"Escaping overrides: `join` escapes every item of the iterable; `replace` escapes only its `new` argument (not `old`); `ljust`, `rjust`, and `center` escape `fillchar`; `__add__`/`__radd__` escape the other operand; `__mod__` escapes each interpolated value; `format`/`format_map` escape fields via `EscapeFormatter`. Non-escaping overrides merely re-wrap the result in the class: `strip`, `lstrip`, `rstrip`, `removeprefix`, `removesuffix`, `partition`, `rpartition`, plus `split`/`rsplit`/`splitlines`, `__getitem__`, `capitalize`, `title`, `lower`, `upper`, `swapcase`, `casefold`, `translate`, `expandtabs`, `zfill`, `__mul__`/`__rmul__`. Since 3.0.0 those search-like methods do not escape their argument because they are conceptually linked to `in`, `find`, `index`, which never escaped their arguments either.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:170-186","src/markupsafe/__init__.py:257-311","CHANGES.rst:55-60"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
