    import markupsafe
from markupsafe import Markup
assert str(Markup('x-y').replace('-', '<')) == 'x&lt;y'
assert str(Markup('a').rjust(3, '<')) == '&lt;&lt;a'
assert str(Markup('|').join(['<a>', Markup('<b>')])) == '&lt;a&gt;|<b>'
assert str(Markup('<a>').lstrip('<')) == 'a>'
assert str(Markup('<p>').removeprefix('<')) == 'p>'
assert [str(v) for v in Markup('<a> <b>').split()] == ['<a>', '<b>']
"},{"id":"g2","kind":"general","question":"`escape` tests `type(s) is str` rather than `isinstance(s, str)`. What does this buy, and how are str subclasses and proxy objects handled as a result?","key":"The identity check is the fast path for the most common case — a plain `str` is sent straight to `_escape_inner` without the `__html__` lookup or a `str()` conversion. `type(s)` is used instead of `s.__class__` because a proxy object may report the `__class__` of the value it proxies (fix from 3.0.1); such a proxy therefore falls through to the generic path and is escaped via `str(s)`. Any `str` subclass likewise skips the fast path: `Markup` (and anything else with `__html__`) is returned unescaped through `Markup(s.__html__())`, while a subclass without `__html__` is escaped after `str(s)` — which is why `_escape_inner(str(s))` must tolerate `str(s)` returning a subclass instance (3.0.2 fix).","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:35-45","CHANGES.rst:24-40"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
    sys.path.insert(0, 'src')
    import markupsafe
from markupsafe import escape, Markup
class Proxy:
    def __init__(self, v):
        self._v = v
    @property
    def __class__(self):
        return self._v.__class__
    def __str__(self):
        return str(self._v)
p = Proxy('<x>')
assert isinstance(p, str) and type(p) is not str
assert str(escape(p)) == '&lt;x&gt;'
class Sub(str):
    def __str__(self):
        return self
assert str(escape(Sub('<x>'))) == '&lt;x&gt;'
assert str(escape(Markup('<x>'))) == '<x>'
"},{"id":"g3","kind":"general","question":"What does `Markup(object, encoding=None, errors="strict")` do with each argument, and what is the trap when passing bytes without an encoding?","key":"`Markup.__new__` first checks `hasattr(object, "__html__")` and, if present, replaces `object` with `object.__html__()` — so wrapping an HTML-aware object marks its HTML output safe. If `encoding is None` it calls `str.__new__(cls, object)`, i.e. plain `str()` conversion with no escaping at all; otherwise it calls `str.__new__(cls, object, encoding, errors)` to decode a bytes-like object. The trap: `Markup(b"<b>hi")` with no encoding yields the repr-ish text `b'<b>hi'` rather than decoded text; you must pass e.g. `Markup(b"<b>hi", "utf-8")`. More generally the constructor never escapes — anything passed in is declared safe, so passing untrusted data here defeats the protection.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:122-131"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
    sys.path.insert(0, 'src')
    import markupsafe
from markupsafe import Markup
assert str(Markup(b'<b>hi', 'utf-8')) == '<b>hi'
assert str(Markup(b'<b>hi')) == "b'<b>hi'"
assert str(Markup('<script>')) == '<script>'
class H:
    def __html__(self):
        return '<img src="x">'
assert str(Markup(H())) == '<img src="x">'
"},{"id":"g4","kind":"general","question":"How do `escape` and `escape_silent` differ for `None`, and what does `soft_str` guarantee?","key":"`escape(None)` has no fast path and no `__html__`, so it escapes `str(None)` and returns `Markup('None')` — the literal word "None" appears in output. `escape_silent` returns an empty `Markup()` for `None` (its only special case) and otherwise just delegates to `escape`; it is meant for optional values. `soft_str` returns the object unchanged if it is already a `str` instance — preserving a `Markup` so it will not be escaped a second time — and otherwise returns `str(s)`; note the isinstance check means any str subclass is passed through as-is.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:48-81"],"check":"import sys
