"},{"id":"g5","kind":"general","question":"How does the package decide between the C speedups and the pure-Python implementation, and what is guaranteed to be identical between them?","key":"`src/markupsafe/__init__.py` does `try: from ._speedups import _escape_inner / except ImportError: from ._native import _escape_inner` at import time — a single module-level binding, chosen once; if the C extension was not built (setup.py falls back to a pure-Python build on compile failure or on PyPy/Jython/GraalVM) the native version is used transparently. Both implement the same contract: replace exactly `&`→`&amp;`, `>`→`&gt;`, `<`→`&lt;`, `'`→`&#39;`, `"`→`&#34;` and nothing else, returning the text unchanged when no such characters occur (the C version then just increfs and returns the input object). Everything else — `Markup`, `escape`'s `__html__` handling — is pure Python either way.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:7-10","src/markupsafe/_native.py:1-8","src/markupsafe/_speedups.c:74-98","setup.py:54-82"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
    sys.path.insert(0, 'src')
    import markupsafe
from markupsafe._native import _escape_inner as native
assert native("&><'\"") == '&amp;&gt;&lt;&#39;&#34;'
assert str(markupsafe.escape("&><'\"")) == native("&><'\"")
assert native('plain text') == 'plain text'
"},{"id":"g6","kind":"general","question":"What exactly does `Markup.striptags` promise, and in which situations does it leave markup-looking text behind?","key":"It removes HTML comments first (repeatedly deleting from `<!--` through the next `-->`), then removes tags (from each `<` through the next `>`), then collapses all whitespace runs to single spaces via `" ".join(value.split())`, and finally returns `unescape()`d text. Comments are handled before tags so a comment containing a tag does not end early. If a `<!--` has no matching `-->`, or a `<` has no matching `>`, the loop breaks and that remainder is kept verbatim (e.g. `Markup("a<b").striptags() == "a<b"`). The return value is a plain `str` with entities decoded, so it is *not* safe markup: re-inserting it into HTML without escaping can reintroduce tags such as `<script>`.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:199-228"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
    sys.path.insert(0, 'src')
    import markupsafe
from markupsafe import Markup
assert Markup('<!-- a <b> c -->x<i>y</i>&amp;z').striptags() == 'xy&z'
assert Markup('a<b').striptags() == 'a<b'
assert Markup('Main &raquo;\t<em>About</em>').striptags() == 'Main \u00bb About'
assert type(Markup('<i>x</i>').striptags()) is str
"},{"id":"g7","kind":"general","question":"Describe the three-step dispatch in `EscapeFormatter.format_field` and the error it raises for a class that defines `__html__` but not `__html_format__`.","key":"For each substituted field: (1) if the value has `__html_format__`, it is called with the format spec and its return value used; (2) otherwise, if the value has `__html__`, it is called — but if a non-empty `format_spec` was given, a `ValueError` is raised with the message "Format specifier {spec} given, but {type(value)} does not define __html_format__. A class that defines __html__ must define __html_format__ to work with format specifiers."; (3) otherwise Python's default `string.Formatter.format_field` is used with `str(format_spec)`. In every case the result is finally passed through `str(self.escape(rv))`, so plain values get escaped while `Markup`/`__html__` output passes through unchanged.","lines":["src/markupsafe/__init__.py:332-354","docs/formatting.rst:13-27"],"check":"import sys
try:
    import markupsafe
except ImportError:
    sys.path.insert(0, 'src')
    import markupsafe
from markupsafe import Markup
class OnlyHtml:
    def __html__(self):
        return Markup('<foo>')
class Both(OnlyHtml):
    def __html_format__(self, spec):
        return Markup('<FORMAT>')
assert str(Markup('{0}').format(OnlyHtml())) == '<foo>'
assert str(Markup('{0}').format(Both())) == '<FORMAT>'
assert str(Markup('{0}').format('<x>')) == '&lt;x&gt;'
try:
    Markup('{0:>10}').format(OnlyHtml())
except ValueError as e:
    assert 'does not define __html_format__' in str(e), e
else:
    raise AssertionError('expected ValueError')
"}]}
